    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d190-a3">

                <pb facs="0337" n="225" sameAs="frus:frus1907p1/pg225"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Rockhill to Consul-General
                    Martin</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1907-07-08"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">July 8,
                    1907</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Replying to your No. 312 of the 19th ultimo
                    inclosing copy of a letter from His Imperial Japanese Majesty’s consul at Hankow
                    with reference to the extension of the Japanese concession at Hankow, I have to
                    inform you that I see no objection to the compliance on the part of the
                    Americans with the request therein contained, to produce their title deeds at
                    the Japanese consulate for registration.</p>
                <p>In reply to the letter of the Japanese consul you should inform him that you have
                    given the notification requested, but that the United States reserves full
                    extraterritorial rights over American-owned property situated in the concession
                    extension, as well as over American owners of such property.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. W. Rockhill.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
